1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Distance is the fewest edges on a path between two vertices, not the shortest straight line.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Breadth-first search from vertex 0 labels every vertex with that count: 0, then 1, then 2, then 3, then 4.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The drawing misleads: vertex 6 looks close to vertex 0 and is four edges away.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Everything in this lesson is read from one table of these counts.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The eccentricity of a vertex is the largest entry in its own row: its distance to the farthest vertex.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Row 0 reads 0 1 2 3 4 3 4, so the eccentricity of vertex 0 is 4.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Row 2 reads 2 1 0 1 2 1 2, so the eccentricity of vertex 2 is 2 — it is never more than two edges from anything.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Eccentricity is a worst case taken from one vertex. Every vertex has one.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Read the seven eccentricities as one list: 4, 3, 2, 3, 4, 3, 4.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The largest is the diameter, 4. The smallest is the radius, 2.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The vertices attaining the radius form the centre — here only vertex 2. Those attaining the diameter form the periphery: 0, 4 and 6.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Four numbers from one list. Radius is a minimum, diameter a maximum, and neither is an average.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
For any connected graph, the radius is at least half the diameter and at most the diameter.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The lower bound: two vertices at distance d are each within r of a centre vertex, so d is at most 2r.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
The upper bound is attained: a six-cycle has diameter 3 and radius 3, and every vertex is in its centre.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A graph whose radius equals its diameter is called self-centred. Cycles and complete graphs are.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
On a tree the floor is always reached: the radius equals half the diameter, rounded up.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
So an odd diameter forces two adjacent centre vertices, and an even diameter forces exactly one.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
All 18248 labelled trees on 2 to 7 vertices were checked here: no exception, and every two-vertex centre is an edge.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The centre is also the middle of any diameter path. That is a tree fact, and it fails on general graphs.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Peeling finds the centre with no distance table: remove every leaf, then repeat on what is left.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
On the eight-vertex path the first round removes both ends, and three rounds leave vertices 3 and 4.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The same rule on the seven-vertex tree leaves the single vertex 2, in two rounds.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
One pass over the edges, so linear time: no all-pairs table is built.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
A diameter can be found with two searches: sweep from any vertex, then sweep from the farthest one reached.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
On every one of the 18248 trees the two sweeps return the true diameter.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
On the diamond — four vertices, five edges — it returns 1 where the diameter is 2.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Sweep from 0 reaches 1 first, and 1 is one step from 0. The far pair, 2 and 3, is never visited as a start.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
First limit: on a general graph a centre vertex need not lie on any diameter path.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
In this five-vertex graph the diameter is 3 and the radius 2, and vertex 2 is in the centre but on no diameter path.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Second limit: a disconnected graph has no finite eccentricity, so it has no radius and no diameter.

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Third limit: centre, centroid and median are three different vertices. Here the centre is 3 and 4; the centroid and the median are only 3.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
GATE CSE 2025 Set 1, Q43: a 100-vertex graph with diameter 30, and any breadth-first-search tree of it.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The height of a BFS tree is the eccentricity of its root, and the diameter is at most twice any eccentricity — so the height is at least 15.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
GATE CSE 2021 Set 1, Q20: add an edge between every pair at distance two. The diameter becomes half the old one, rounded up.

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Both answers were recomputed here on the page's own graphs, not copied from a key.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
One procedure: build the distance table, read each row maximum, then take the smallest and the largest.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
If the graph is a tree, skip the table: peel leaves until one or two remain, and take half the diameter rounded up.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Traps one and two: halving the diameter on a graph with a cycle, and assuming the centre lies on a diameter path.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Traps three to five: a leaf's eccentricity is not the height, two sweeps are not general, and a disconnected graph has none.

